## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental triangle counting under streaming edge additions; evaluates ability to design batched analysis and compare to full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Incremental graph algorithms
- Degree-based edge orientation
- Wedge counting and triangle updates
- Amortized analysis
- Space-time trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function addEdge(u,v,adj,deg,tri){
  if (deg[u] > deg[v]) [u,v] = [v,u];
  let cnt = 0;
  for (const w of adj[u]){
    if (adj[v].has(w)) cnt++;
  }
  tri.total += cnt;
  adj[u].add(v); adj[v].add(u);
  deg[u]++; deg[v]++;
}
```

## Further Discussion
This batched approach significantly outperforms rebuilding from scratch, which would require O(n³) time in the worst case for triangle enumeration. The incremental method achieves near-linear preprocessing and maintains efficient updates, making it practical for large-scale dynamic graphs where edges arrive continuously.

## Why This Is Asked
A practical dynamic 2x2 submatrix counter seizes local updates; analytic challenge: bound work per update and handle edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Local neighborhood counting; incremental updates
- Amortized O(B) per batch; worst-case O(B) with 4 checks per update
- Space: store full matrix and current count; avoid full scan

## Code Example
```javascript
function updateCell(mat, n, i, j, val, cur) {
  const old = mat[i][j];
  if (old === val) return cur;
  for (let di = -1; di <= 0; di++) {
    for (let dj = -1; dj <= 0; dj++) {
      const r = i + di, c = j + dj;
      if (r >= 0 && c >= 0 && r + 1 < n && c + 1 < n) {
        const before = mat[r][c] & mat[r+1][c] & mat[r][c+1] & mat[r+1][c+1];
        // after flip not yet applied
        // compute new values after applying val to mat[i][j]
        const a = (r === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r][c];
        const b = (r+1 === i && c === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c];
        const c2 = (r === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r][c+1];
        const d = (r+1 === i && c+1 === j) ? val : mat[r+1][c+1];
        const after = a & b & c2 & d;
        if (before !== after) {
          cur += (after ? 1 : -1) * 1; // adjust
        }
      }
    }
  }
  mat[i][j] = val;
  return cur;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to counting 3x3 all-ones squares with O(1) per update? 
- How do you handle non-binary updates or weighted counts?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a memory-bounded streaming estimator for cardinality, a core real-time analytics task.

## Key Concepts
- Cardinality estimation in streams
- HyperLogLog-like sketches and register invariants
- Trade-offs: memory vs. accuracy vs. update speed

## Code Example
